  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 159 AM EST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nFor the early portion of the week, mid level zonal flow gradually \ngives way to some weak mid level ridging through Monday and into \nTuesday. At the surface, what is left of the very weak frontal \nboundary washes out as high pressure builds in from the north. This \nwill allow for a light to moderate east northeasterly wind flow to \nremain in place during this time frame. With this area of high \npressure in place, combined with the weak mid level ridging aloft, \nthere will be enough subsidence taking place to support mostly dry \nconditions across the region on Monday and Tuesday. However, with \nthe east northeasterly wind flow continuing, some lower level \nmoisture may still linger over the region which could keep a very \nlow end chance of showers in place mainly over the Atlantic waters\nand east coast. Any shower that does develop will be low topped \nand rather short lived. High temperatures on Monday and Tuesday \nwill generally range from the lower 80s across the Lake Okeechobee\nregion and east coast metro areas to the mid to upper 80s over \nSouthwest Florida. \n\nHeading into the middle of the week, the weather pattern will start \nto change a bit as a mid level shortwave pushes across the Gulf \nCoast states and into Northern Florida on Wednesday into Wednesday \nnight. At the surface, high pressure will gradually start to push \nfurther east into the western Atlantic. The latest global and \nensemble guidance remain in relatively good agreement keeping \nthis shortwave off to the north of the region, however, it will \nstart to draw in some deeper moisture over South Florida. This \ncould increase the chances for showers during the middle of the \nweek especially during the afternoon hours during peak diurnal \nheating. High temperatures on Wednesday will generally range from \nthe lower 80s across the Lake Okeechobee region and the east coast\nmetro areas to the mid to upper 80s across Southwest Florida. \n\nThe uncertainty in the forecast towards the end of the week rises as \nthe latest global and ensemble guidance show a deeper trough \npushing over the Eastern Seaboard during this time frame. At the \nsurface, guidance is showing signs of a developing area of low \npressure over the Mid-Atlantic region during the Thursday and \nFriday time frame. The cold front associated with this system \ncould move into and pass through the region towards the end of \nthe week which would increase the chances of showers and \nthunderstorms during this time frame. However, guidance still does\nremain in disagreement in regards to exact timing of the front as\nwell as its intensity as it approaches the region towards the end\nof the week. With the front nearby, the latest forecast increases\nthe chances of showers and thunderstorms for the end of the week.\nBehind the front, there will be the potential for a much cooler\nair mass to push into the region for the following weekend. More\nto follow on this as the details become clearer as the week \nprogresses.",
